  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on complete bus-line removal as the sole disruption model, which may not capture more common partial service reductions or localized infrastructure failures.",
      "what": "This study evaluates the structural and functional robustness of public transit networks in Mexico City, Rio de Janeiro, and Buenos Aires under simulated complete bus-line removal scenarios. Using open infrastructure and mobility data, the researchers measured network fragmentation, trip feasibility, and increased travel costs as lines were progressively dismantled. The analysis revealed a strong alignment between structural and functional robustness across all three cities, with Rio de Janeiro demonstrating the highest structural resilience due to its high line redundancy.",
      "why": "By combining topological network metrics with actual mobility data, this approach moves beyond purely structural vulnerability assessments to quantify the real-world cost and feasibility of trips during major transit disruptions."
    }
